<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Farabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 870–950</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abu Nasr al-Farabi, Alpharabius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Farabi was a philosopher of the Islamic world, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and spent his last years at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, where he died around 950. Called the 'Second Teacher' after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, he wrote commentaries on Greek logic, a treatise on the virtuous city and influential works on music. He shaped the philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-farabi.jpg" alt="Al-Farabi" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr.Nostalgic · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpharabius_in_Liber_Chronicarum_1493_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
